examination of revolutions in the Iberian and Italian peninsulas, Sicily and Greece in the 1820s that reveals a popular constitutional culture in the South After the turbulent years of the Napoleonic Wars and the Congress of Vienna’s attempt to guarantee peace and stability across Europe, a new revolutionary movement emerged in the southern peripheries of the continent. In this groundbreaking study, Maurizio Isabella examines the historical moment in the 1820s when a series of simultaneous uprisings took the quest for constitutional government to Portugal, Spain, the Italian peninsula, Sicily and Greece. Isabella places these events in a broader global revolutionary context and, decentering conventional narratives of the origins of political modernity, reveals the existence of an original popular constitutional culture in southern Europe.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-southern-europe-in-the-age-of-revolutions-maurizio-isabella.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.007,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0.356,"traits":[{"id":"academic","score":0.38,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Princeton University Press / Princeton University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Princeton University Press / Princeton University Press"]},{"id":"narrative","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"quest\""]},{"id":"accessible","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"In the 1820s, simultaneous uprisings across Portugal, Spain, the Italian peninsula, Sicily, and Greece demonstrate an original popular constitutional culture in southern Europe, decentering conventional accounts of political modernity’s origins.","confidence":0.66},"academicOrient